{"id": "38e8398d-206c-4d5d-a9a2-e75e015fe300", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T06:42:44.886709", "source": "user", "parent_id": "360710ea-4efe-450e-bd38-16375e42f5f5", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved with explicit resolutions to the six uncertainties so the final design can be self-consistent without further clarification. The design-only constraint stands: do NOT describe a run, do NOT fabricate measured numbers from the sandbox. Where numeric estimates appear, they must be clearly labeled as extrapolations from published QuaRot / LLaMa-2 references, grounded to cited tables/figures, and explicitly distinguished from any local measurements (of which there will be none). Produce the final experiment design in the stage=result report with the following structure and resolutions: (A) Title, objective restatement, and a one-paragraph non-technical summary. (B) Research questions — keep the five RQs you proposed (quality, prefill performance, decode performance, attribution of cost/quality to weight-only / activation / KV-cache / rotation components, MHA-vs-GQA scaling between 7B and 13B). (C) Theoretical / mechanistic background subsection: a concise description of QuaRot's transformations — Hadamard rotations inserted around q k v o and gate up down projections and around residual streams, the resulting invariance property that allows online K/V rotation per decode step, and why end-to-end W4A4 with K4V4=4-bit is the natural headline cell. (D) Cell matrix (the experimental grid) — keep your seven cells, with the following resolutions to my uncertainties baked in as defaults: U1 → primary QuaRot cell is W4A4 with K4V4=4-bit, with W4A4 + K4V4=FP16 included as an ablation to attribute KV-cache 4-bit contribution; U2 → the FP16 control is rotated-FP16 (rotation is a methodological requirement of QuaRot, so overhead is part of the cost being measured); baseline unrotated FP16 is included only as a sanity reference; U3 → note GPU unavailable locally; latency/throughput attribution is done via a calibrated analytical cost model parameterized by memory bandwidth and compute throughputs from published 4-bit matmul / Hadamard-kernel figures, not by execution; U4 → Wikitext-103 perplexity + lm-evaluation-harness 0-shot on ARC-c / HellaSwag / PIQA / WinoGrande, matching the QuaRot paper's harness to within ±1 model; U5 → primary metric is wall-clock latency broken into prefill tokens/s and decode tokens/s with mean / p50 / p95 / p99, secondary metrics are memory footprint and throughput; energy is out of scope unless trivially derivable; U6 → report qualitative cell-by-cell deltas with orders-of-magnitude estimates explicitly attributed to the cited reference and clearly labeled as extrapolations. (E) Metrics and measurement methodology: define prefill latency as wall time to first token for prompts of length L ∈ {512, 1024, 2048, 4096}, decode latency as mean and tail per-token latency over N=256 generation steps with KV cache populated, KV-cache footpring via a closed-form size model (S_kv = 2 × L_gen × n_kv × d_head × layers × bytes_per_elem) distinguishing 7B MHA (n_kv=32) from 13B GQA (n_kv=8), peak GPU memory via a layer-wise activation + parameter + KV accounting. (F) Profiling strategy: separately account for Hadamard-rotation kernel cost (acts on a flat 2D tensor with a fixed-size random Hadamard matrix; cost scales with token count, runs once per linear in prefill and once per token for online K/V in decode) from the W4A4 / W4A16 matmul cost; report a per-phase decomposition table for the headline cell (QuaRot W4A4) showing rotation overhead vs matmul overhead in both prefill and decode. (G) Attributions subsection: a qualitative table mapping each quality delta and each performance delta to one of {weight-only quantization, activation quantization, KV-cache quantization, rotation overhead} so the reader can see which lever drives each result. (H) Controls and confounders: identical calibration data, identical prompts, identical generation length, identical decoding algorithm (greedy or sampling with fixed seed — pick one and justify), identical hardware assumption in the analytical model; explicitly call out confounders such as batching, KV-cache layout (contiguous vs paged), framework (PyTorch + custom CUDA vs FasterTransformer vs vLLM or analogous 4-bit backend). (I) Feasibility: confirm the design fits in your stated remaining requests, confirm no network / GPU / dependency installation is needed for the design report itself, and call out which cells are mandatory vs optional (mandatory = the seven cells above on 7B and 13B at one sequence length; extensions to 70B or extra sequence lengths are optional and should be marked as such). (J) Risk register and limitations: bullet list the top three risks to validity, e.g. (i) extrapolation from published numbers may miss kernel-engineering improvements, (ii) tail-latency percentiles are extremely sensitive to memory-bandwidth contention and the model may not capture this, (iii) MHA-vs-GQA scaling on KV cache assumes identical GQA factor to published LLaMa-2 13B; verify before final write-up. (K) Final cell-by-cell expected-findings sketch: a small table with one row per cell and columns for expected quality Δ, expected prefill throughput Δ, expected decode throughput Δ, expected memory Δ, and the dominant mechanism — each cell filled with a directional expectation (improves / degrades / neutral) and a one-line justification. Produce the stage=result report accordingly; ground every numeric estimate in a specific QuaRot or LLaMa-2 reference and label it as an extrapolation; do not present anything as a measurement performed in the sandbox.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 37}
